AE-02 — AC-005 real-process variant attempt and structural finding.
Goal: exercise the real BaseServerManager.startAttempt() -> waitForPortToBeFree() ->
PortConflictError / ServerStatusManager ERROR / no-kill / recovery path in a real,
test-owned Electron app process (not just the unit-level real-socket test, IMP-01).

Steps run:
1) Built the worktree Electron app and started an isolated instance (test-owned data root
   /tmp/abp-api-e2e-probe/iso-data-root, test-owned port 56321, never 29695):
   'pnpm isolated-app start --build --data-root <dir> --server-port 56321' -> ok, RUNNING.
2) Stopped it ('isolated-app stop <id>'): port released, data root kept (ownsDataRoot=false,
   so the tool never deletes it).
3) Held port 56321 with a real [::] dual-stack listener (node net.createServer().listen(port))
   — the incident's exact holder shape.
4) Attempted 'isolated-app start' again on the same port/data-root while held: it failed
   immediately with SERVER_PORT_IN_USE. Reading instanceLifecycle.mjs's selectServerPort(),
   this is the CLI wrapper's OWN pre-launch portAvailable() guard, not the product's
   BaseServerManager conflict path — it never launches Electron at all.
5) To reach the product's own code, launched the built AutoByteus.app binary directly
   (bypassing the CLI wrapper) with the exact e2e-profile env/args
   (AUTOBYTEUS_ELECTRON_LAUNCH_PROFILE=e2e, AUTOBYTEUS_ELECTRON_SERVER_PORT=56321,
   AUTOBYTEUS_ELECTRON_DATA_ROOT=<dir>, --remote-debugging-port, --autobyteus-isolated-instance)
   while the same [::] holder was still listening. Result: electron/main.ts's bootstrap()
   calls assertEmbeddedServerListenerPortAvailable() for the 'e2e' profile BEFORE
   BaseServerManager/ElectronApplication even exist; that function
   (electron/launch-profile/e2eLaunchPreflight.ts) is a single assertBackendPortAvailable()
   check with no retry, so it rejects immediately and bootstrap() calls app.exit(1).
   See the captured log below: '[electron-bootstrap] Startup failed: listen EADDRINUSE:
   address already in use 0.0.0.0:56321' — a hard, non-recoverable exit, not the product's
   PortConflictError / ServerStatusManager ERROR / recoverable-screen path.

Structural finding: resolveElectronLaunchProfile() (electronLaunchProfile.ts) has exactly
two profiles. 'e2e' (the only test-owned, port/data-root-overridable profile — used by
every isolated-app mechanism) takes the fast-fail preflight above for a port conflict,
by design, so isolated/e2e test runs fail fast instead of engaging the production
retry/recovery UX. 'production' is the ONLY profile whose launch reaches
BaseServerManager.startAttempt() -> waitForPortToBeFree() -> PortConflictError ->
ServerStatusManager ERROR (the real BEH-004/005 conflict-recovery path AC-005 describes),
and it is hard-wired to PRODUCTION_EMBEDDED_SERVER_PORT (29695) and the real canonical
user data path (electronLaunchProfile.ts: 'production' rejects AUTOBYTEUS_ELECTRON_SERVER_PORT/
AUTOBYTEUS_ELECTRON_DATA_ROOT entirely). There is no test-owned way to reach that orchestration
in a real full desktop-app process without using port 29695 / the real app data directory,
which QR-002 and TESTING.md Rule 2 forbid. This is a pre-existing structural property of the
launch-profile design, not something this ticket introduced or could safely work around.

Consequence: AC-005's real-process variant is Not Tested / Infeasible at the full-app
orchestration level, for a reason outside this ticket's control. The real-socket unit test
(IMP-01, BackendPortAvailability.spec.ts) already exercises the exact detection function
(assertBackendPortAvailable/waitForBackendPort) both profiles ultimately call, with the
incident's [::] holder shape, the no-kill assertion, and the post-close recovery assertion —
see its own evidence (evidence/IMP-01-electron-targeted.log).

Cleanup: holder process killed (port 56321 confirmed free); isolated instance stopped
(serverPortReleased=true, controlPortReleased=true); 'isolated-app list' shows no remaining
records; the directly-launched probe process exited on its own (app.exit(1)) and left no
process; the test-owned data root was removed. No effect on the user's installed app
(verified running throughout at its own port 29695 / ~/.autobyteus, untouched).

